    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "2\n3\n1 2\n2 3\n5\n3 4\n5 3\n2 3\n1 2",
        "output": "8\n13",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: Color vertex 111 red, vertex 222 blue, and vertex 333 green. Then,\n\nscore(1)\\text{score}(1)score(1) depends on the closest blue and green vertices to 111, since it's red.\nThese are 222 and 333 respectively, at distances 111 and 222. So, score(1)=1+2=3\\text{score}(1) = 1+2 = 3score(1)=1+2=3.\nscore(2)\\text{score}(2)score(2) depends on the closest red and green vertices to 111, since it's blue.\nThese are 111 and 333 respectively, at distances 111 and 111. So, score(2)=1+1=2\\text{score}(2) = 1+1 = 2score(2)=1+1=2.\nscore(3)\\text{score}(3)score(3) depends on the closest blue and red vertices to 111, since it's green.\nThese are 222 and 111 respectively, at distances 111 and 222. So, score(3)=1+2=3\\text{score}(3) = 1+2 = 3score(3)=1+2=3.\n\nThe sum of scores is 3+2+3=83+2+3 = 83+2+3=8. It can be proved that this is optimal.\nTest case 222: Suppose we color vertex 333 red, vertex 222 blue, and vertices 1,4,51, 4, 51,4,5 all green. Then,\n\nThe scores of the vertices can be seen to be [3,2,2,3,3][3, 2, 2, 3, 3][3,2,2,3,3].\nThe beauty is the sum of all of these, which is 131313.\n\nIt can be proved that no valid coloring attains a beauty less than 131313."
      }
    ]
